dan domenech is the executive director  of the American Association of School  Administrators he's leading a task force  that is assisting schools all around the  country with their plans to reopen  safely he joins us now good morning to  you sir you know the stakes could not be  higher here we heard some teachers talk  about how it's a life-or-death choice  for them going back to the classroom so  how are you advising schools to weigh  the benefit of in-school instruction  against that risk that could really be  catastrophic well good morning Tony and  absolutely we have been talking to  superintendents from all over the  country for the last couple of months  trying to come up with how to reopen  schools safely and that is the primary  concern safety so we are concerned about  the safety of the students but also the  safety of the staff so we have a number  of plans and you're seeing that  happening right now in terms of what  districts have announced is going to  happen when school opens there are  basically three plans the one is to come  back as if nothing has happened which by  the way some districts are considering  and I don't know why the second one is  this hybrid model where basically  students come in for a couple of days a  week than their home so that this way  you can try to get to all of the kids in  the building and the third one which  we're also beginning to see particularly  in districts around this Washington DC  area and other parts of the country is  that it's not safe yet and therefore  remote learning is going to continue but  safety is the primary factor we're using  the CDC guidelines primarily in terms of  outlining for us what is safe we want to  make sure students are safe and we want  to make sure that staff is safe yeah dan  in those three categories that you just  outlined I'm curious with the tens of  millions of students all around the  country waiting for an outcome what  who's in what category and how big are  the three categories like how many  states and school districts might be  going back with very few precautions how  many in that middle position and how  many not going back at all it's  interesting Tony how things are changing  I can tell you that a month ago in  talking to many of our colleagues along  the southern belt states at that point  they were considering in just opening  schools or come on  all in many cases without any  precautions in terms of spacing or  facemask that's beginning to change now  and we're beginning to see even  governor's in those states like Kansas  is an example indicating that face masks  are mandatory postponing the opening of  schools the primary thing here is you  want to open schools according to what  was faced to from the whites a couple of  months ago and that is when the epidemic  the rate of infection is going down not  going on that's and when you do that  that you have the social spacing and all  the other precautions yeah but there may  be only there most states right now have  are either stable or going up in case  count so if we go by that that indicator  I mean most places are not going to open  I have a question from Jessica in  Indiana a viewer who also believed as a  teacher and she wants to know simply why  are we even talking about going to the  classroom at all why sacrifice in her  view the health of teachers and students  and maybe families when elearning is an  option it is an option and you're seeing  more and more districts doing that  announcing that that they are going to  open but remotely the key thing here are  the conditions the rate of the epidemic  in that community if it's going up you  don't want to open if it is going down  if it's minimal if things are being are  stabilizing then opening following the  precautions listed by the CDC are  important that six feet spacing between  children wearing of masks and all of  those other factors so it really has to  be determined by the community but it  has to be when it's safe to open not the  condition would that we see in many  states were the infection rate is  sky-high  yeah if schools make the investment to  change the environment and reopen and  then they get a case does that mean they  shut down completely what happens then  absolutely that's what the guidelines  say if there's a case in the building of  student or staff we're more than that  that building has to shut down it has to  be sanitized and it has to be a period  of time before the schools can come back  so you can almost predict that when  schools do open even with the safest of  precautions the minute there's the case  they're going to have to shut down again  I want to get in a question from Tabitha  Ross Roy who happens to be the Teacher  of the Year this past year and she says  look we know things gonna be different  but what can teachers do to help  students feel like this is a normal year  in some sense well you know what I don't  think there's any way you can help  students feel that this is a normal year  because it's not but the reality is that  these children these students have gone  through a lot of issues stress problems  and first and foremost the teachers have  to deal with that they have to talk to  their students they have to make sure  that they're comfortable they have to  hear their issues and their problems yes  there's been academic loss there's been  learning loss no question about that  but first and foremost in addition to  the safety and welfare of the students  is their emotional status and teachers  do a very good job in doing that yeah  they sure do and we should remind our  viewers that this is not a federal  decision it is a state and local  decision right down to the level of the  school district dan Dominic thank you  very much for walking us through your  position
 

